PUBLIC RESPONSE: F. None of the above

All five forms are attested as end-rhyme words in the Book of the Duchess.

* wente is the regular rhymed past of *wenan/going* and is coupled in the poem with shente / bente etc.;
* here is regularly used as a line-ending and is rhymed with there / were / dere in the dream vision;
* fool is a normal line-ending and is rhymed with cool / tool etc.;
* hool = ME *hool* ‘whole, hale’ is the form Chaucer uses, and it is the exact rhyme partner he sets against fool/cool in the couplet work of the poem;
* countour “counter, counterpane” occurs as a line-ending and is rhymed with tour(e) / flour(e) etc. in the description of the palace/garden.

Hence every item in A–E is a word Chaucer does make a rhyme with in the Book of the Duchess, so the correct choice is None of the above.

