E. Countour

The other four items are all attested as line-final rhyme words in the octosyllabic couplets.

* wente is the normal -ente past, e.g. wente / sente / shente / bente / entente;
* here is a very frequent dream-vision line ending, here / there / were / dere / fere / mere;
* fool is the normal -ool rhyme of the self-mockery passages, fool / cool / wool / tool etc.;
* hool / hoole is the ME spelling of ‘whole/hale’ and is the conventional -ool partner that Chaucer sets with fool in the Duchess. The -ool family is regularly closed in the poem.

Countour is the odd one out. The French-derived *countour ‘counter, reckoner’ does occur in the vocabulary of the Duchess, in the catalogue of the Black Knight’s household and in the Ceyx/Alcyone narration, but it is used medially / in prose-like description and is never placed as the terminal rhyme of a couplet in the extant Hengwrt/Ellesmere readings of the poem. It is therefore the only item in A–E that Chaucer does not make a rhyme with in The Book of the Duchess.

